server: Report directory changes on macOS through FSEvents.

Wine learns about changes in a directory from inotify, which macOS doesn't
have, so on a Mac ReadDirectoryChangesW never completed. Programs that talk
to a helper through files in a watched folder waited for nothing: the Epic
Games Launcher prepares a game's install folder in a second, elevated copy of
itself, which reports back by writing a file into such a folder; the launcher
never saw the file and gave the install up after 50 seconds (DP-06), and the
player saw the install dialog close with nothing downloading.

On macOS wineserver now watches each directory a program waits on with an
FSEvents stream. FSEvents calls back on a dispatch queue of its own, so the
new fsevents.c only queues the events and wakes the poll loop through a pipe;
change.c matches them to the waiting directories on the server thread, drops
what lies below a watch without subtree, and turns FSEvents' flags into
added, removed, renamed (old and new name paired) and modified records.
Dropped events wake the program without data, which it gets as
STATUS_NOTIFY_ENUM_DIR.

FSEvents' flags pile up: a file created a few seconds ago comes as "created"
with every later write, a renamed one as "renamed", and a folder removed soon
after its creation as created and removed. Whether the item still exists
decides whether it went away, and each watch remembers the items (name and
inode, the newest 1024) it has announced, so that a write into a new file is
a modification and not the file being added again, as on Windows.

Wine's kernel32:change and ntdll:change tests go from 108 failures to 14 on
macOS. The rest wait 0 or 100 ms right after a change, sooner than FSEvents
reports through fseventsd, or get a removal their directory saw just before
the watch began.

